INFERENCE WEEKLY
Friday, March 6, 2026

Hello. The week in running models, for people who pay the bill.

THE PRICE CUT, ONE WEEK ON
Aurelian's 40% cut on Sable-3 has not been matched. What it has done is move traffic: the two gateways that publish routing stats both show Sable-3 large taking share from the other two frontier models on cost-sensitive workloads. If you are on an annual commitment with another lab, this is your leverage at renewal, and the account managers know it.

BATCH TIERS: who is actually using them
We asked. Of 60 teams that replied, 14 route any traffic to a batch tier, and those 14 route a median 35% of their tokens there. The other 46 said "on the roadmap." The savings are 50% on the routed volume. The blocker in every case was the same: someone has to write the queue.

ROUTING GATEWAYS COMPARED
We ran the same 10,000-request workload through four routing gateways. Cost spread: 1.7x between best and worst, most of it from how aggressively they downgrade easy requests to small models. Latency spread: 1.3x. The cheapest one was also the least transparent about why it chose a model. Full table on the site.

FIVE LINKS
1. A practical guide to structured-output modes across providers, with the edge cases.
2. The open-source project that turns provider usage exports into one cost dashboard.
3. A short paper on why output tokens cost more, from the hardware up.
4. A team's story of cutting their model bill 60% with three routing rules.
5. The interconnect lead-time data, if you want to know why your cluster is late.

ONE NUMBER: $0.12. Per million input tokens on the model that now handles most classification traffic we can see. Two years ago that work cost $3.

That's the week. Reply with what you are running.

